course: dictionary
lesson: 1702
title: Many meanings of one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings. Churn is a good example. The words around it, called its context, tell you which meaning the writer wants. Look at what is being churned: milk, water or a stomach.
explain: «The boat churned up the water.» Here churned means moved and stirred the water roughly, so it became foamy and messy. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «My stomach churned before the test.» Your stomach cannot be shaken like cream, so churn must mean something else. It means you felt sick, often because you were worried. Clues from the whole sentence show you the right meaning.
fact: Churn can be a verb or a noun. As a verb it is an action, and as a noun it is a thing.
fact: A churn is a container. People used to shake or stir cream in a churn to make butter.
fact: «The stomach churns it in strong acid» means the stomach moves and mixes food around.
fact: «To churn out» means to make lots of something quickly, often without care.
fact: «European robins once followed wild boar and deer that churned up the soil» means the animals stirred up the earth.
exercise: «The boat churned up the water.» What does churned mean here? | Sang happily to the fish | Painted the boat blue | Stopped the boat completely | *Moved and stirred the water roughly
exercise: Ella says, «My stomach churned before the test.» What does it mean? | You fell fast asleep | You ate a big dinner | *You felt sick, often from worry | You were very hungry
exercise: «Grandma poured the cream into the churn.» What is a churn? | A kind of small boat | *A container for cream or milk, used to make butter | A large wooden spoon | A type of bread
test: «The factory churns out hundreds of toys every day.» What does churns out mean? | *Makes lots of things quickly, often without care | Carefully builds one toy at a time | Sells toys in a shop | Throws away broken toys
test: Why might a writer choose churn in «The storm churned the sea»? | It means the sea was calm and still | It means the sea had frozen solid | It means the sea was only a small pond | *It shows the sea was moving wildly and violently
exam: «The weir made the water churn round and round, so the swimmer was held under.» What does churn mean here? | Turn into butter | Freeze solid in the cold | Stay perfectly still and calm | *Move and stir wildly
